[Music]  welcome back  former minneapolis police officer derek  shovin will be sentenced for the murder  of george floyd next month  anthony levy shows us why the  prosecution wants a harsher sentence  while shoving's team says  the law doesn't allow for it the  sentencing of derek shovin is really the  last big battle to be fought at the  trial court level  and it's no surprise that the  prosecution and the defense  want different things in minnesota  sentences are determined by guidelines  that take into account a defendant's  criminal history  chauvin doesn't have a record so under  the guidelines he would be sentenced to  150 months which is 12 and a half years  but prosecutors want a sentence that  exceeds the guideline  it's called an upward departure they  want possibly as long as 40 years  the judge has the discretion to depart  upward to give a greater sentence  although he does not have to judge peter  cahill will decide whether aggravating  factors existed in this case that would  allow for an upward departure  prosecutors say those factors are george  floyd being particularly vulnerable  they claim that floyd was treated with  particular cruelty and that chauvin  abused his position of authority  the prosecutors go on to say that the  crime was committed  with three or more people and happened  in the presence of children  meanwhile chauvin's attorney argues  against a greater sentence  he says floyd was not particularly  vulnerable because of his size  eric nelson says abuse of a position of  authority is not a recognized  aggravating factor in minnesota  he also says the other defendants  haven't been convicted  and the state has not proven the three  other officers intentionally aided  chauvin in a crime  he also notes the children walking by  and stopping to witness the arrest  is not sufficient grounds for an upward  departure not only would the conviction  itself  be um reviewed by by a court of appeals  uh but if cahill does depart upward and  give a greater sense  that would also be an issue on appeal  derek chovin is scheduled to be  sentenced on june 25th  for law and crime daily i'm angenette  levy  and be sure to tune in to the law and  crime network this friday  for an hour-long special on the derek  shoven trial hosted by angenette levy  and thank you for joining us here at  long crime daily we'll see you next time  as we discuss justice  in america  [Music]  you

